{{nihongo|'''Kenzan Mihashi'''|三橋 兼山|Mihashi Kenzan}} (23 June 1857 – 1 August 1922) was a Sanese politician who served as the 10th [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from 1916 to 1922. The first "commoner" Prime Minister, Mihashi's long tenure represented the high point of [[Go-Shōkō Democracy]], expanding suffrage, promoting labor unions, and attempting to rein in the military budget. His assassination on 1 August 1922 by a right-wing ultranationalist signaled the end of the liberal interlude and the beginning of the military's ascendancy over civilian government.

== Early life ==
Kenzan Mihashi was born on 23 June 1857 in [[Shirakawa]], [[Nagayama Prefecture]], the son of a rural schoolteacher. His family was of commoner stock—a background that set him apart from the samurai-born genrō who had dominated the early Saito state. Mihashi's humble origins would later become a central part of his political identity, as he styled himself as the first "commoner" prime minister and champion of the people against the old oligarchy.

Mihashi attended the prefectural school in [[Nagayama (city)|Nagayama]] before winning a scholarship to [[Okami Imperial University]], where he studied economics. He was an outstanding student, graduating at the top of his class in 1880. His academic brilliance attracted the attention of the Ministry of Finance, which recruited him as a junior economist.

After several years in the bureaucracy, Mihashi grew disillusioned with the hanbatsu-dominated civil service and left government in 1886 to pursue a career in business. He managed a series of successful commercial ventures in Nagayama and Toyozawa, including a sake brewing concern, a silk reeling operation, and a regional bank that financed small merchants and foresters. The ventures made him a modest fortune and, more importantly, gave him a network of contacts in the commercial and professional classes that would later form the backbone of his political base.

== Political career ==
Mihashi entered politics in the 1890s, joining the [[Rikken Seiyūkai]] shortly after its founding. He was elected to the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]] in 1894 and quickly became known as a principled advocate for democratic reform and fiscal responsibility. His background in both the bureaucracy and the private sector gave him a unique perspective on the relationship between the state and the economy.

Mihashi rose through the Seiyūkai's ranks, becoming one of the party's leading voices on economic policy. He advocated for progressive taxation, expanded public education, and the development of labor protections—positions that put him at odds with the party's more conservative faction but earned him a devoted following among the growing urban middle class.

By 1910, Mihashi had emerged as the leader of the Seiyūkai's reformist wing. He challenged the party's old guard for the leadership and, after a bitter internal struggle, was elected party president in 1912. His victory represented a generational shift within the Seiyūkai, as the party moved from its origins as a hanbatsu-aligned establishment party toward a more genuinely popular and democratic orientation.

== Premiership (1916–1922) ==
Mihashi was appointed Prime Minister on 18 December 1916, following the resignation of [[Reijiro Masuko]]. His appointment was a watershed: for the first time, a commoner—a man without samurai lineage or military connections—held the premiership. The genrō council had opposed his appointment, but the political parties and the public demanded a democratic figure, and the Emperor overrode the council's objections.

The Mihashi government's agenda was the most ambitious reform program since the [[Saito Restoration]]. Key achievements included:

* '''Universal manhood suffrage (1919):'''
[[File:Mihashi Universal Suffrage 1919.png|thumb|right|200px|The Revised Election Law of 1919 extended the franchise to all male citizens over 25]]
The Mihashi government passed the Revised Election Law, which abolished the tax qualification for voting and extended the franchise to all male citizens over the age of 25. This reform tripled the electorate and fundamentally transformed the character of the Diet, giving the common people a voice in government for the first time.

* '''Labor union legislation (1918):''' The Labor Union Act recognized the right of workers to organize, bargain collectively, and strike. While the act contained significant restrictions—including a prohibition on public sector unions and political strikes—it represented a landmark in the recognition of labor rights and was fiercely opposed by the conservative establishment.

* '''Military budget reforms (1917–1920):''' Mihashi attempted to rein in the military budget, which had grown to consume over 40% of government expenditure. He reduced the Army's operational budget by 15% and redirected the savings to education and infrastructure. These reforms were deeply unpopular with the military and would ultimately contribute to his downfall.

* '''Economic stabilization (1917–1918):''' Drawing on his expertise as an economist, Mihashi implemented a series of monetary reforms that stabilized the Sanese yen and reduced inflation. He also negotiated a new trade agreement with [[Moselle]] that lowered tariffs on Sanese exports and secured access to Mosellan capital markets.

=== Conflict with the military ===
The Mihashi government's relationship with the military was the central political conflict of the premiership. The Army and Navy viewed Mihashi's budget cuts as an existential threat, and military officers repeatedly attempted to undermine his government through leaks, public criticism, and political intrigue.

The most serious confrontation came in 1920, when the Army Chief of Staff publicly denounced Mihashi's defense policies in a speech to the [[House of Officers of Okaiken|House of Officers]]
[[File:Mihashi Military Budget Conflict 1920.png|thumb|right|200px|The Army Chief of Staff's denunciation of Mihashi's defense policies in the House of Officers]]
—a breach of military protocol that the prime minister was unable to punish. The incident demonstrated the fundamental weakness of civilian government under the [[Saito Constitution]]: the military's constitutional right of "independent command" ({{nihongo|tōsuiken|統帥権}}) placed its operations beyond the Diet's authority, making it impossible for a prime minister to enforce civilian control.

Mihashi's response to the 1920 incident became famous. Rather than seek a confrontation the constitution would not let him win, he took the unprecedented step of appearing personally before the House of Officers to defend his budget in open session—the first prime minister to do so under the Saito system. His closing remark, that "a nation is defended first by its schoolteachers and second by its soldiers," was reported across the country and made him a hero to the urban press even as it hardened the officer corps against him. The [[Okami Times]] of the era called it "the commoner's rebuke"; Army journals called it "the merchant's insult."

Despite these challenges, Mihashi maintained his reform agenda until the end of his life. He continued to push for further reductions in military spending and for the expansion of democratic institutions, even as the opposition from the military and the right wing grew increasingly violent.

=== Foreign policy ===
The Mihashi government's foreign policy was characterized by a commitment to international cooperation and a cautious approach to the Shendan. Mihashi believed that the nation's long-term security depended on economic strength and diplomatic engagement rather than military expansion, and he sought to reduce tensions with the Illypnian colonial powers through negotiation.

The government's most significant diplomatic achievement was the Mosellan-Sanese Trade Agreement of 1917, which lowered tariffs on Sanese exports and secured access to Mosellan capital markets. The agreement was a lifeline for the Sanese economy, which had been struggling under the burden of military spending, and it established Moselle as the nation's most important trading partner—a relationship that would endure for decades.

In the Shendan, Mihashi pursued a policy of commercial diplomacy, seeking to expand Sanese economic influence through trade agreements and investment rather than military force. The Shendan Commercial Commission's operations were expanded, and the government negotiated a series of bilateral trade agreements with Shendan states that significantly increased Sanese commercial activity in the region. However, the Illypnian colonial powers—particularly Volisania and Breisland—continued to impose restrictive trade regulations on Sanese vessels, and Mihashi's diplomatic efforts to resolve these disputes achieved only limited success.

The government also sought to manage the growing tensions with [[Sansi]] over the northern territories and Kwangju. Mihashi authorized the renewal of the Sansian fishing rights agreement negotiated under the Saionji government, which reduced tensions along the maritime boundary, but he resisted the Army's demands for a military buildup along the northern border. The Kwangju advisory mission was maintained, but Mihashi refused to expand it, arguing that increased involvement in Kwangju would inevitably lead to conflict with Sansi.

The Mihashi government also pursued closer relations with [[Zong]], signing the Mitoyagi-Zong Commercial Agreement of 1920, which provided a framework for Sanese investment in Zong's developing economy. The agreement was the precursor to the more comprehensive Mitoyagi-Zong Treaty that would later be negotiated under the Ashikaga administration.

=== Assassination ===
[[File:Mihashi Assassination 1922.png|thumb|right|200px|The site of Mihashi's assassination outside the Diet building on 1 August 1922]]
On 1 August 1922, Mihashi was assassinated by a right-wing ultranationalist outside the Diet building. The assassin, a former Army officer named Horiguchi Tesshin, claimed that Mihashi's policies had weakened the nation's defenses and betrayed the spirit of the Saito Restoration. Horiguchi was arrested at the scene and later sentenced to life imprisonment, though he was widely celebrated in right-wing circles as a patriot.

Mihashi's assassination sent shockwaves through the political establishment. It demonstrated that the forces of reaction were willing to use violence to achieve their ends, and it marked the beginning of the end of the Go-Shōkō Democracy. No subsequent prime minister would attempt to challenge the military's budget with the same determination.

=== Funeral and public reaction ===
Mihashi was given a state funeral on 10 August 1922, the first for an assassinated prime minister of the Saito era. An estimated 400,000 mourners lined the procession route through central Okami, and the Emperor—breaking with protocol to honor the commoner prime minister—sent the Lord Keeper of the Privy Seal as his personal representative. Labor unions, whose right to exist Mihashi had secured four years earlier, marched in the cortege in an act of defiance that right-wing newspapers condemned and liberal ones celebrated as "the commoner's guard of honor."

The government's response to the assassination revealed the weakness of the state it led. The [[Korekiyo Takahashi|Takahashi government]] that succeeded Mihashi passed the Public Security Preservation Bill in an attempt to crack down on ultranationalist societies, but Army-aligned peers in the House of Officers watered it down until it was largely symbolic. Horiguchi's life sentence was never commuted, but neither was the network of officers and civilian societies that had produced him ever seriously investigated. Contemporary diaries from the Army Ministry show that the General Staff viewed the assassination with quiet satisfaction—an assessment that did not become public until the archives were opened decades later.

== Legacy ==
Kenzan Mihashi is remembered as the most significant democratic reformer of the Go-Shōkō era. His expansion of the franchise, recognition of labor rights, and attempts to establish civilian control over the military represented the high-water mark of democratic governance in Okaiken. His assassination marked the beginning of the end of the liberal interlude, as the military and right-wing forces increasingly used violence to intimidate democratic politicians.

The contrast between Mihashi's reformist vision and his violent death has made him a symbol of the democratic aspirations that were thwarted by the militarization of the 1930s. His economic policies—particularly his monetary reforms—were continued by his successor [[Korekiyo Takahashi]], and the Bank of Okaiken's independence that he had championed remained a cornerstone of Sanese economic policy.

Mihashi's universal manhood suffrage reform had the most enduring impact, permanently expanding the electorate and laying the groundwork for the democratic institutions that would eventually be restored after the war. His legacy as the "commoner prime minister" who challenged the old oligarchy continues to inspire democratic movements in Okaiken to this day.

After the Great Adonian War, Mihashi underwent a broad historical rehabilitation. The post-war constitution's guarantee of universal suffrage was consciously modeled on the Revised Election Law of 1919, and the drafters of the Quiet Revolution-era settlement invoked his premiership as proof that democratic government was not a foreign import but a Sanese achievement twice made. The Mihashi Memorial in [[Okami]], erected near the site of his assassination in 1961, bears his famous House of Officers remark: "A nation is defended first by its schoolteachers and second by its soldiers."

== Personal life ==
Mihashi married Chiyo, the daughter of a Nagayama merchant family, in 1884; she died of tuberculosis in 1888, leaving him a widower with a young son. He remarried in 1891, to Sugi, a former schoolteacher who shared his commitment to public education and who became a prominent advocate for girls' schooling in her own right. He had three children: Kenji, who followed his father into the family bank and later into the House of Commoners; Tamae, who became one of the first women to head a normal school in Nagayama Prefecture; and Yūji, who died in the Great Adonian War.

Mihashi was a lifelong diary-keeper, and the twelve volumes of his diaries, published between 1952 and 1968, are among the most important primary sources for the Go-Shōkō era. In the final entry, written on the morning of his death, he noted only the weather and a meeting scheduled for that afternoon.

== Electoral history ==
{{see also|Okaiken general election, 1917|Okaiken general election, 1920}}
Mihashi sat in the House of Commoners continuously from 1894 until his death in 1922. Under the tax-qualified franchise of his early career his majorities were narrow; after his own Revised Election Law of 1919 tripled the electorate, his 1920 re-election—won on a platform of school construction and veteran retraining—was the largest personal victory of his career.

